            syllabus = listOf("Problem Formulation: State Spaces, Transition Models & Goal Tests", "Uninformed Search: Breadth-First, Depth-First & Uniform Cost Search (Dijkstra)", "Informed Search: Greedy Best-First & A* Algorithm (f(n) = g(n) + h(n))", "Heuristic Properties: Admissibility (h(n) ≤ h*(n)) & Monotonic Consistency", "Adversarial Search: Game Trees, Minimax Algorithm & Alpha-Beta Pruning Bounds"),
            coreFormulas = listOf("A* Evaluation Function: f(n) = g(n) + h(n) (where g(n) = path cost, h(n) = estimated cost to goal)", "Admissibility Criterion: 0 ≤ h(n) ≤ h*(n) (never overestimates the true cost)", "Monotonic Consistency (Triangle Inequality): h(n) ≤ c(n, a, n') + h(n')", "Alpha-Beta Pruning Condition: Prune when α ≥ β (α = max lower bound, β = min upper bound)", "A* Space Complexity: O(b^d) where b is branching factor and d is goal depth"),
            part1Title = "State Spaces, Heuristic Evaluation & Adversarial Game Trees",
            part1Content = "Artificial intelligence problem solving begins by abstracting real-world domains into formal state space graphs. A state space comprises an initial state s₀, actions A(s) applicable in state s, a transition model Result(s, a), a goal test predicate, and a path cost function c(s, a, s'). The search problem seeks an optimal sequence of actions transforming s₀ into a goal state. Uninformed search strategies explore state spaces without domain knowledge: Breadth-First Search (BFS) is complete and finds optimal shallowest solutions using O(b^d) memory, while Depth-First Search (DFS) sacrifices optimality for minimal O(bm) linear stack memory.\n\nInformed (heuristic) search utilizes domain-specific heuristic functions h(n) estimating the cheapest cost from node n to the nearest goal. The A* algorithm evaluates candidate frontier nodes using f(n) = g(n) + h(n), where g(n) is the exact cost accumulated from the start node and h(n) is the heuristic estimate. If h(n) is admissible—meaning it never overestimates the true minimal cost to the goal (h(n) ≤ h*(n))—tree-search A* is guaranteed to return the mathematically optimal path.\n\nGraph-search A* avoids redundant re-expansion of previously visited states by maintaining an explored set. Optimal graph search requires heuristic consistency (monotonicity): for every node n and successor n' generated by action a, h(n) ≤ c(n, a, n') + h(n'). Consistency satisfies the triangle inequality, guaranteeing that f(n) values monotonically non-decrease along any path, eliminating the need to re-open nodes in the closed set.\n\nIn multi-agent environments, Adversarial Search models strategic two-player zero-sum perfect-information games (such as Chess and Go). The Minimax algorithm recursively computes optimal decisions by maximizing utility for the active agent while assuming the opponent minimizes it. Alpha-beta pruning dramatically accelerates minimax without altering the final decision by maintaining running thresholds α (highest value MAX is guaranteed) and β (lowest value MIN is guaranteed), pruning subtrees as soon as α ≥ β.",
            part2Title = "Analytical Formulations, Worked Examples & Applications",
            part2Content = "Exact Equations & Governing Rules:\n• A* Frontier Priority: f(n) = g(n) + h(n)\n• Alpha-Beta Bounds: α = max(α, v) at MAX nodes; β = min(β, v) at MIN nodes; Prune when α ≥ β\n• Manhattan Distance Heuristic (Grid): h(n) = |x₁ - x₂| + |y₁ - y₂|\n\nWorked Numerical Example:\nProblem: In a route-planning grid, start is S(0, 0), goal is G(3, 3). Step cost is 1 per orthogonal move. Two nodes are on the frontier: Node A at (1, 1) with g(A) = 2, and Node B at (0, 2) with g(B) = 2. Using Manhattan distance h(n) = |x - 3| + |y - 3|, which node does A* expand first?\nStep 1: Compute heuristic for Node A(1, 1):\n• h(A) = |1 - 3| + |1 - 3| = 2 + 2 = 4.\n• f(A) = g(A) + h(A) = 2 + 4 = 6.\nStep 2: Compute heuristic for Node B(0, 2):\n• h(B) = |0 - 3| + |2 - 3| = 3 + 1 = 4.\n• f(B) = g(B) + h(B) = 2 + 4 = 6.\nStep 3: Tie-breaking: A* can expand either node with f = 6.\nStep 4: Suppose Node A expands to successor C(2, 1): g(C) = 2 + 1 = 3.\n• h(C) = |2 - 3| + |1 - 3| = 1 + 2 = 3.\n• f(C) = g(C) + h(C) = 3 + 3 = 6.\nStep 5: Node C expands to D(3, 1): g(D) = 4, h(D) = |3-3| + |1-3| = 2 ⇒ f(D) = 6. Finally reaching G(3, 3) with g(G) = 6, h(G) = 0 ⇒ f(G) = 6 (Optimal path of length 6).\n\nReal-World Applications:\nA* search is the universal pathfinding algorithm in autonomous mobile robotics, GPS satellite navigation (Google Maps route optimization), and video game character AI navigation meshes. Minimax with alpha-beta pruning forms the evaluation backbone of competitive chess engines (Stockfish).",
            examTip = "University Exam Tip: For A* to be optimal in tree search, h(n) must be ADMISSIBLE (never overestimates). For A* to be optimal in graph search without node re-opening, h(n) must be CONSISTENT (satisfies triangle inequality). In alpha-beta pruning, prune the moment α ≥ β.",
            mcqs = listOf(
