    private fun createChapter2(): CatalogChapter {
        return CatalogChapter(
            chapterId = 402,
            chapterOrder = 2,
            title = "Knowledge Representation & Logic",
            estimatedMinutes = 25,
            summary = "Propositional logic, First-Order Logic (FOL), quantifiers, unification, resolution refutation proofs, and forward/backward chaining inference engines.",
            syllabus = listOf("Propositional Logic: Syntax, Semantics, Entailment (α ⊨ β) & Model Checking", "First-Order Logic (FOL): Predicates, Functions, Quantifiers (∀, ∃) & Domain Semantics", "Inference Rules: Generalized Modus Ponens, Skolemization & Conjunctive Normal Form (CNF)", "Robinson's Resolution Refutation Proofs & Most General Unifiers (MGU)", "Deductive Engines: Forward Chaining (Data-Driven) vs Backward Chaining (Goal-Driven)"),
            coreFormulas = listOf("Logical Entailment: α ⊨ β ⇔ In all models where α is true, β is also true", "Resolution Rule: (A ∨ B) ∧ (¬B ∨ C) ⊢ (A ∨ C) (Resolvent)", "Skolemization (Existential Quantifier Elimination): ∃y P(x, y) ⇒ P(x, S_k(x))", "De Morgan's FOL Duals: ¬(∀x P(x)) ≡ ∃x ¬P(x) and ¬(∃x P(x)) ≡ ∀x ¬P(x)", "Generalized Modus Ponens: From p₁', p₂', ..., p_n' and (p₁ ∧ ... ∧ p_n ⇒ q), infer qθ where p_i'θ = p_iθ"),
            part1Title = "First-Order Logic, Resolution Refutation & Inference Engines",
            part1Content = "Knowledge Representation enables autonomous AI systems to maintain explicit, declarative world models and deduce new conclusions. While propositional logic evaluates Boolean variables, First-Order Logic (FOL) enriches representation by modeling objects, properties (predicates), functions, and relations over variable domains. Universal quantification (∀x) asserts a statement holds across all domain elements, whereas existential quantification (∃x) asserts existence for at least one element.\n\nInference engines deduce new knowledge from an established Knowledge Base (KB). Logical entailment KB ⊨ α formally means that every model satisfying KB also satisfies sentence α. Model checking validates entailment by testing truth across all 2^n valuations, but quickly becomes computationally intractable. Syntactic inference mechanisms operate directly on symbolic formulas without enumerating full truth tables.\n\nAutomated theorem proving relies fundamentally on Robinson's Resolution Refutation Principle. To prove KB ⊨ α, the system assumes the negation ¬α, converting the combined sentence KB ∧ ¬α into Conjunctive Normal Form (CNF)—a conjunction of disjunctions of literals. First-order CNF conversion standardizes variables, eliminates implications, moves negations inward, and replaces existential quantifiers with Skolem functions. Unification identifies a Most General Unifier (MGU) substitution θ making conflicting literals identical: resolving (A ∨ B) with (¬B ∨ C) produces the resolvent (A ∨ C). If resolution derives the empty clause (contradiction □), the theorem is formally proved.\n\nExpert systems and logic programming languages (such as Prolog) utilize restricted Horn clauses (disjunctions with at most one positive literal). Forward chaining is a data-driven strategy that continuously fires rules whose premises are satisfied in the KB, deriving new facts until a query is reached. Backward chaining is a goal-driven strategy that starts with the query, searching backward through implication rules to find supporting premises in the KB.",
            part2Title = "Analytical Formulations, Worked Examples & Applications",
            part2Content = "Exact Equations & Governing Rules:\n• CNF Conversion: Eliminate implications (A ⇒ B ≡ ¬A ∨ B); Apply De Morgan's; Distribute ∨ over ∧\n• Resolution Step: (P ∨ Q) and (¬P ∨ R) yield resolvent (Q ∨ R)\n• Unification: MGU(P(x, f(a)), P(b, y)) = {x/b, y/f(a)}\n\nWorked Numerical Example:\nProblem: Given Knowledge Base:\n1. King(x) ∧ Greedy(x) ⇒ Evil(x)\n2. King(John)\n3. Greedy(John)\nProve Evil(John) using Resolution Refutation.\nStep 1: Convert KB to CNF:\n• Clause 1: ¬King(x) ∨ ¬Greedy(x) ∨ Evil(x)\n• Clause 2: King(John)\n• Clause 3: Greedy(John)\nStep 2: Negate the goal to be proved: Add ¬Evil(John) to the set.\nStep 3: Resolve Clause 1 with Clause 2 with substitution θ₁ = {x/John}:\n• Resolvent 4: ¬Greedy(John) ∨ Evil(John).\nStep 4: Resolve Resolvent 4 with Clause 3 (Greedy(John)):\n• Resolvent 5: Evil(John).\nStep 5: Resolve Resolvent 5 with the negated goal ¬Evil(John):\n• Resolvent 6: Empty Clause (□ Contradiction!).\nConclusion: Since assuming ¬Evil(John) derives a contradiction, Evil(John) is logically entailed by the Knowledge Base!\n\nReal-World Applications:\nFirst-order logic and automated reasoning power modern technology infrastructure. Formal verification engines (Z3 SMT Solver) mathematically prove hardware circuit correctness and software security invariance (preventing buffer overflow bugs). Semantic Web ontologies (OWL/RDF) organize healthcare biomedical knowledge graphs, while compliance audit systems automatically verify enterprise regulatory frameworks.",
            examTip = "University Exam Tip: In resolution refutation proofs, always start by NEGATING the query sentence you wish to prove. If your resolution steps derive the empty clause (□), the proof is complete and valid.",
            mcqs = listOf(
                QuizQuestionDto(
                    id = 4009,
                    questionNumber = 1,
                    question = "What is the goal of a Resolution Refutation proof in automated logic theorem proving?",
                    options = mapOf("A" to "Add the negation of the query to the KB and derive an empty clause (contradiction)", "B" to "Prove that all predicates evaluate to True simultaneously", "C" to "Construct an infinite parse tree", "D" to "Find the maximum eigenvalue of the knowledge matrix"),
                    correctOption = "A",
                    explanation = "Resolution refutation asserts ¬Goal; deriving the empty clause (contradiction □) mathematically proves that the Goal is strictly entailed by the KB."
                ),
                QuizQuestionDto(
                    id = 4010,
                    questionNumber = 2,
                    question = "What is the result of resolving the two clauses (P ∨ Q) and (¬P ∨ R)?",
                    options = mapOf("A" to "Q ∨ R", "B" to "P ∧ R", "C" to "¬Q ∨ ¬R", "D" to "P ∨ Q ∨ R"),
                    correctOption = "A",
                    explanation = "The resolution rule cancels the complementary literals P and ¬P, producing the resolvent clause (Q ∨ R)."
                ),
                QuizQuestionDto(
                    id = 4011,
                    questionNumber = 3,
                    question = "What is Skolemization in First-Order Logic?",
                    options = mapOf("A" to "Eliminating existential quantifiers by replacing variables with Skolem constants or functions", "B" to "Converting disjunctions into implications", "C" to "Removing all universal quantifiers", "D" to "Inverting a truth table"),
                    correctOption = "A",
                    explanation = "Skolemization removes existential quantifiers ∃y P(x, y) by substituting y with a Skolem function S(x) dependent on the universally quantified variables."
                ),
                QuizQuestionDto(
                    id = 4012,
                    questionNumber = 4,
                    question = "What is the Most General Unifier (MGU) for literals Knows(John, x) and Knows(y, Mary)?",
                    options = mapOf("A" to "θ = {y / John, x / Mary}", "B" to "θ = {x / John, y / Mary}", "C" to "Unification is impossible", "D" to "θ = {x / y}"),
                    correctOption = "A",
                    explanation = "Substituting y with John and x with Mary yields identical expressions Knows(John, Mary) for both literals."
                ),
                QuizQuestionDto(
                    id = 4013,
                    questionNumber = 5,
                    question = "Which inference strategy starts with initial facts and applies rules iteratively to derive new conclusions?",
                    options = mapOf("A" to "Forward Chaining (Data-Driven)", "B" to "Backward Chaining (Goal-Driven)", "C" to "Minimax Strategy", "D" to "Simulated Annealing"),
                    correctOption = "A",
                    explanation = "Forward chaining is a data-driven approach that triggers inference rules whose antecedents are satisfied by known facts until the target hypothesis is generated."
                ),
                QuizQuestionDto(
                    id = 4014,
                    questionNumber = 6,
                    question = "What is a Horn clause?",
                    options = mapOf("A" to "A clause containing at most one positive literal", "B" to "A clause with strictly negative literals only", "C" to "A tautology that is always true", "D" to "A clause containing no variables"),
                    correctOption = "A",
