    private fun createChapter3(): CatalogChapter {
        return CatalogChapter(
            chapterId = 403,
            chapterOrder = 3,
            title = "Supervised Machine Learning",
            estimatedMinutes = 25,
            summary = "Linear regression, logistic regression, gradient descent optimization, cost functions (MSE, Cross-Entropy), regularizations (L1/L2), and bias-variance trade-off.",
            syllabus = listOf("Supervised Learning Framework: Features, Targets & Loss Functions", "Linear Regression: Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) & Normal Equation", "Gradient Descent Optimizers: Batch, Mini-Batch & Stochastic (SGD)", "Logistic Regression: Sigmoid Hypothesis, Log-Odds & Binary Cross-Entropy", "Model Regularization: Ridge (L2), Lasso (L1) & Bias-Variance Decomposition"),
            coreFormulas = listOf("Mean Squared Error (MSE): J(w) = (1 / (2m)) * ∑_{i=1}^m (h_w(x^(i)) - y^(i))²", "Normal Equation: w = (X^T X)⁻¹ X^T y", "Sigmoid Activation Function: σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(-z))", "Binary Cross-Entropy Loss: L(w) = - (1/m) * ∑ [y log(h(x)) + (1 - y) log(1 - h(x))]", "L2 Ridge Regularization: J_reg(w) = J(w) + (λ / (2m)) * ∑ w_j²; L1 Lasso: J_reg(w) = J(w) + (λ / m) * ∑ |w_j|"),
            part1Title = "Parametric Models, Loss Formulations & Gradient Optimization",
            part1Content = "Supervised machine learning algorithms learn predictive mapping functions f: X → Y from labeled training sets D = {(x^(i), y^(i))}_{i=1}^m. Regression predicts continuous real-valued targets, while classification assigns discrete class labels. In linear regression, the model assumes a linear relationship h_w(x) = w^T x. The Mean Squared Error (MSE) cost function measures the average squared residual discrepancy between predictions and ground-truth targets, forming a convex parabolic surface with a unique global minimum.\n\nOptimizing parameters can be achieved analytically or iteratively. Analytically, setting the gradient of MSE to zero yields the Normal Equation w = (X^T X)⁻¹ X^T y, which is exact but computationally expensive for high-dimensional feature spaces due to O(d³) matrix inversion. Iteratively, Gradient Descent updates weights in the direction of steepest descent: w ← w - η ∇J(w), where learning rate η modulates step size. Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) and Mini-Batch SGD compute gradient updates over small subsets, accelerating convergence across massive datasets.\n\nClassification uses Logistic Regression to model the posterior probability P(y = 1 | x). The linear predictor z = w^T x is mapped to the range (0, 1) via the non-linear Sigmoid activation function σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(-z)). Because applying MSE to sigmoid outputs creates non-convex surfaces prone to local minima, logistic regression optimizes Binary Cross-Entropy (Log Loss), derived from Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE), guaranteeing convexity.\n\nModel generalization requires balancing the Bias-Variance Trade-off. High bias (underfitting) occurs when a model lacks capacity to capture underlying trends; high variance (overfitting) occurs when a model memorizes training noise. Regularization constrains model complexity by adding penalty terms to the loss function. Ridge Regression (L2 regularization) shrinks weights toward zero via squared penalties, mitigating multicollinearity. Lasso Regression (L1 regularization) uses absolute penalties, driving non-essential coefficients strictly to zero to perform automatic feature selection.",
            part2Title = "Analytical Formulations, Worked Examples & Applications",
