            part1Title = "Clustering Paradigms, Density Reachability & Dimensionality Reduction",
            part1Content = "Unsupervised machine learning analyzes unlabeled data matrices X ∈ R^(m × d) to discover underlying cluster groupings, manifolds, and latent representations. Without ground-truth supervisory target labels y, algorithms extract natural geometric partitions and intrinsic dimensions based on distance metrics and probability densities.\n\nK-Means clustering partitions m observations into K distinct clusters C₁, ..., C_K minimizing within-cluster sum-of-squares (inertia). Lloyd's algorithm alternates between two steps: the Assignment step, where each point is assigned to its nearest centroid μ_k using Euclidean distance, and the Update step, where centroids are recomputed as the arithmetic mean of all assigned points. The algorithm converges to a local minimum forming convex Voronoi polyhedral cells. The optimal number of clusters K is determined using the Elbow Method (inflection point of inertia decay) and the Silhouette Coefficient, which compares intra-cluster compactness a(i) with nearest-cluster separation b(i).\n\nHierarchical clustering constructs a multi-level nested tree of clusters visualized via a dendrogram. Agglomerative (bottom-up) clustering initializes every point as a single-element cluster, iteratively merging the closest pair of clusters according to a linkage criterion: Single Linkage (minimum distance, prone to chaining), Complete Linkage (maximum distance, compact clusters), Average Linkage, or Ward's Linkage (minimizing total within-cluster variance increase).\n\nDensity-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise (DBSCAN) overcomes the fundamental limitations of K-Means by identifying clusters of arbitrary geometric shape while filtering isolated noise outliers. Points are classified based on radius ε and threshold MinPts: a Core Point contains at least MinPts within its ε-neighborhood; Border Points fall within an ε-neighborhood of a core point but have fewer than MinPts neighbors; all remaining points are Noise. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) performs linear dimensionality reduction by projecting zero-centered data onto eigenvectors of the covariance matrix corresponding to the largest eigenvalues, maximizing preserved variance.",
