            syllabus = listOf("Computer Vision Challenges: Parameter Explosion in Fully Connected Networks", "2D Convolution Operation: Kernels, Feature Maps, Stride (S) & Padding (P)", "Spatial Pooling: Max Pooling, Average Pooling & Translation Invariance", "Receptive Field Mathematics & Output Spatial Dimension Formulas", "Deep Architectures: LeNet-5, VGG, and ResNet Residual Skip Connections"),
            coreFormulas = listOf("Convolution Output Dimension: O = ⌊(W - K + 2P) / S⌋ + 1", "Same Padding Condition: P = (K - 1) / 2 (for odd kernel size K with stride S = 1)", "2D Convolution Formula: (I * K)(i, j) = ∑_m ∑_n I(i - m, j - n) K(m, n)", "Residual Block Formulation: y = F(x, {W_i}) + x (Skip connection solves vanishing gradients)", "Max Pooling Output: O_pool = ⌊(W - P_size) / S_pool⌋ + 1"),
            part1Title = "Convolutions, Spatial Invariance & Deep Residual Architectures",
            part1Content = "Processing high-dimensional visual images using standard fully connected Multi-Layer Perceptrons leads to an immediate parameter explosion. A modest 1000 × 1000 RGB image contains 3 million input values, requiring trillions of weights for a single dense layer, leading to catastrophic overfitting. Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) resolve this by enforcing two fundamental inductive biases: Local Connectivity (neurons connect only to local receptive fields) and Weight Sharing (the same filter kernel convolves across the entire image).\n\nThe 2D convolution operation slides a learnable filter kernel K ∈ R^(k × k) across an input feature map I. At each spatial position, it computes the Frobenius inner product (element-wise multiplication followed by summation) plus a shared bias. Hyperparameters govern spatial geometry: Stride (S) specifies pixel step size, while Padding (P)—such as zero-padding—controls boundary dimensions. 'Same' padding preserves input spatial resolution by setting P = (K - 1)/2 for odd kernel dimensions.\n\nPooling layers progressively downsample spatial dimensions to reduce computational complexity and introduce translation invariance. Max Pooling extracts the maximum activation within local patches (commonly 2 × 2 with stride 2), retaining dominant features while discarding spatial noise. The effective Receptive Field of a neuron—the subregion of the original input image influencing its activation—expands linearly with depth, allowing deeper layers to integrate local edges into semantic parts (eyes, wheels) and complete scene concepts.\n\nAs networks grew deeper (such as VGG-16 and VGG-19), training accuracy saturated and degraded due to vanishing and exploding gradient signals. ResNet (He et al., 2015) introduced identity shortcut skip connections: instead of forcing stacked layers to fit an underlying mapping H(x), layers optimize a residual mapping F(x) = H(x) - x, outputting y = F(x) + x. If an added layer is redundant, backpropagation drives weights toward zero while the identity skip connection passes gradients unimpeded, enabling stable training of networks exceeding 1000 layers.",
            part2Title = "Analytical Formulations, Worked Examples & Applications",
            part2Content = "Exact Equations & Governing Rules:\n• Output Dimension: O = ⌊(W - K + 2P) / S⌋ + 1\n• Total Parameters per Conv Layer: Params = (K_w × K_h × Channels_in + 1) × Channels_out\n• ResNet Residual Identity: y = F(x) + x ⇒ ∂L/∂x = ∂L/∂y * (∂F/∂x + I)\n\nWorked Numerical Example:\nProblem: An input image of size 32 × 32 × 3 (RGB) is passed through a convolutional layer with 16 filters of size 5 × 5, stride S = 1, and zero-padding P = 2. Find the output feature map dimensions and total learnable parameters.\nStep 1: Compute output spatial width and height:\n• Input width W = 32, Kernel size K = 5, Padding P = 2, Stride S = 1.\n• Output dimension O = ⌊(32 - 5 + 2(2)) / 1⌋ + 1 = ⌊(32 - 5 + 4) / 1⌋ + 1 = ⌊31 / 1⌋ + 1 = 32.\n• Spatial dimensions are preserved at 32 × 32 ('Same' padding).\nStep 2: Determine output channels: Equals number of filters = 16.\n• Output feature map volume: 32 × 32 × 16.\nStep 3: Calculate learnable parameters:\n• Each filter has volume: K_w × K_h × Channels_in = 5 × 5 × 3 = 75 weights.\n• Adding 1 bias term per filter: 75 + 1 = 76 parameters per filter.\n• For 16 filters: Total Parameters = 16 × 76 = 1,216 parameters.\nConclusion: Output size is 32 × 32 × 16, requiring exactly 1,216 parameters (vastly fewer than a dense layer's 32×32×16 × 32×32×3 ≈ 50 million weights).\n\nReal-World Applications:\nCNNs are the foundational engine of modern computer vision. They drive autonomous vehicle perception (Tesla, Waymo object detection for pedestrian and lane tracking), medical radiology diagnosis (detecting tumors in MRI and CT scans), facial recognition biometrics, and satellite imagery segmentation.",
