            coreFormulas = listOf("Scaled Dot-Product Attention: Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax((Q K^T) / √(d_k)) * V", "Multi-Head Attention: MultiHead(Q, K, V) = Concat(head₁, ..., head_h) * W^O", "Head Projection: head_i = Attention(Q W_i^Q, K W_i^K, V W_i^V)", "Sinusoidal Positional Encoding: PE(pos, 2i) = sin(pos / 10000^(2i/d_model)), PE(pos, 2i+1) = cos(pos / 10000^(2i/d_model))", "Computational Complexity of Self-Attention: O(n² * d) where n is sequence length"),
            part1Title = "Attention Mechanisms, Transformer Blocks & Autoregressive LLMs",
            part1Content = "Prior to the Transformer architecture, natural language processing relied on sequential recurrent neural networks (RNNs and LSTMs). Because RNNs process tokens sequentially step-by-step (h_t = f(h_{t-1}, x_t)), they suffer from fundamental computational bottlenecks: training cannot be parallelized across tokens, and hidden states struggle to retain long-range dependencies over distant contexts. The landmark paper 'Attention Is All You Need' (Vaswani et al., 2017) discarded recurrence entirely, establishing the Transformer architecture built entirely on self-attention mechanisms.\n\nSelf-attention allows every token in a sequence to attend directly to every other token simultaneously. Each input token embedding is projected into three distinct learned vector spaces via weight matrices: Query (Q = X W^Q), Key (K = X W^K), and Value (V = X W^V). The relevance score between query token i and key token j is computed via dot product Q_i · K_j^T. Dividing by the scaling factor √(d_k) prevents dot products from growing excessively large in high dimensions, which would push softmax activations into saturated regions with near-zero gradients. Applying the Softmax function produces an attention weight matrix whose rows sum to 1.0, computing an attention-weighted combination of the Value vectors.\n\nMulti-Head Attention (MHA) enhances representational power by executing h parallel attention projections with independent parameter matrices W_i^Q, W_i^K, W_i^V. This permits the model to simultaneously attend to information from distinct representation subspaces at different positions (e.g., syntactic subject-verb agreement in one head, semantic coreference in another). Because self-attention is permutation-invariant and lacks inherent awareness of token sequence order, Sinusoidal Positional Encodings PE(pos) or learned positional embeddings are added directly to the input token vectors.\n\nModern Large Language Models (LLMs) branch into distinct Transformer topologies: Encoder-only architectures (such as BERT) utilize bidirectional attention for classification and token extraction; Encoder-Decoder architectures (T5) excel at sequence-to-sequence translation; and Decoder-only autoregressive architectures (such as GPT-4, Llama, and Gemini) employ causal masked attention to predict the next token probability distribution P(w_t | w₁:t-1), scaling gracefully with compute and data according to empirical Chinchilla scaling laws.",
            part2Title = "Analytical Formulations, Worked Examples & Applications",
            part2Content = "Exact Equations & Governing Rules:\n• Attention Equation: Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax((Q K^T) / √(d_k)) * V\n• Softmax Function: softmax(z_i) = e^(z_i) / ∑ e^(z_j)\n• Causal Masking: Add -∞ to upper-triangular positions before softmax to prevent attending to future tokens\n\nWorked Numerical Example:\nProblem: Compute Scaled Dot-Product Attention for a 2-token sequence with Query Q and Key K where Q K^T = [[2, 4], [4, 2]], with key dimension d_k = 4, and Value matrix V = [[1, 0], [0, 1]].\nStep 1: Compute scaling factor: √(d_k) = √4 = 2.\nStep 2: Scale the dot products by dividing by √(d_k):\n• Scaled scores S = (Q K^T) / 2 = [[2/2, 4/2], [4/2, 2/2]] = [[1, 2], [2, 1]].\nStep 3: Apply Softmax row-wise to get attention weights A:\n• Row 1: scores are [1, 2].\n  - e¹ ≈ 2.718, e² ≈ 7.389. Sum = 2.718 + 7.389 = 10.107.\n  - A₁₁ = 2.718 / 10.107 ≈ 0.269.\n  - A₁₂ = 7.389 / 10.107 ≈ 0.731.\n  - Row 1 weights: [0.269, 0.731].\n• Row 2: scores are [2, 1].\n  - By symmetry, Row 2 weights: [0.731, 0.269].\n• Attention Weight Matrix A = [[0.269, 0.731], [0.731, 0.269]].\nStep 4: Multiply attention weights by Value matrix V:\n• V = [[1, 0], [0, 1]].\n• Output Row 1 = 0.269 × [1, 0] + 0.731 × [0, 1] = [0.269, 0.731].\n• Output Row 2 = 0.731 × [1, 0] + 0.269 × [0, 1] = [0.731, 0.269].\nFinal Attention Output: [[0.269, 0.731], [0.731, 0.269]].\n\nReal-World Applications:\nTransformers power generative AI revolutions. Autoregressive foundation LLMs (ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude) generate code and natural language text. Multimodal vision transformers (ViT) replace CNNs in computer vision by treating image patches as token sequences. Protein folding prediction systems (AlphaFold) utilize evoformer attention to predict 3D protein structures directly from amino acid sequences.",
            examTip = "University Exam Tip: Be prepared to derive the scaling factor 1/√(d_k): if components of q and k are independent random variables with zero mean and unit variance, their dot product has mean 0 and variance d_k. Dividing by √(d_k) restores unit variance, preventing softmax saturation!",
            mcqs = listOf(
                QuizQuestionDto(
                    id = 4049,
                    questionNumber = 1,
                    question = "Why does Scaled Dot-Product Attention divide the dot product Q K^T by the square root of key dimension √(d_k)?",
                    options = mapOf("A" to "To prevent large dot products from pushing the softmax function into regions with near-zero vanishing gradients", "B" to "To ensure the output matrix is symmetric", "C" to "To convert the matrix into complex numbers", "D" to "To speed up matrix multiplication by half"),
                    correctOption = "A",
                    explanation = "For large d_k, dot products grow large in magnitude, pushing softmax into extreme regions where gradients are infinitesimally small. Scaling by 1/√(d_k) preserves unit variance."
