            coreFormulas = listOf("Cosine Similarity: cos(θ) = (u · v) / (||u|| * ||v||) = (∑ u_i v_i) / (√(∑ u_i²) * √(∑ v_i²))", "Normalized Euclidean Distance: ||u - v||² = 2 - 2 * cos(θ) (for unit normalized vectors)", "TF-IDF Term Weight: TF-IDF(t, d, D) = TF(t, d) * log(|D| / |{d ∈ D : t ∈ d}|)", "RAG Context Grounding: Prompt_augmented = Context_retrieved + User_Query", "Demographic Parity Fairness: P(Ŷ = 1 | A = 0) = P(Ŷ = 1 | A = 1)"),
            part1Title = "Vector Embeddings, RAG Architecture & Ethical Alignment",
            part1Content = "While Large Language Models possess broad general knowledge encoded in their parametric weights, they suffer from fundamental limitations: factual hallucinations, outdated training cutoffs, and inability to access private enterprise data. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) overcomes these vulnerabilities by decoupling static parametric model memory from dynamic external non-parametric knowledge bases, grounding generations in verifiable retrieved source text.\n\nThe RAG lifecycle consists of three distinct phases: Ingestion, Retrieval, and Generation. During Ingestion, source documents are segmented into overlapping semantic chunks (e.g., 200–500 tokens). A neural embedding model (such as text-embedding-3 or BERT) maps each text chunk into a dense high-dimensional semantic vector space R^d (e.g., d = 768 or 1536). These vector embeddings are indexed in specialized Vector Databases (such as Milvus, Pinecone, or FAISS) utilizing Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) index graphs like Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW).\n\nDuring the Retrieval phase, an incoming user query is embedded into the identical vector space. The vector index executes a nearest neighbor search, ranking document chunks using vector similarity metrics such as Cosine Similarity: cos(θ) = (u · v) / (||u|| ||v||). The top-k most semantically relevant text chunks are extracted and concatenated into an augmented system prompt. In the Generation phase, the LLM synthesizes an authoritative, hallucination-free response grounded directly on the provided context passages, providing inline citations.\n\nAI Ethics and Safety govern the responsible deployment of artificial intelligence. Unsupervised training datasets inherit historical societal biases, causing models to exhibit demographic disparities in hiring, credit, and criminal justice risk scoring. Alignment methodologies—such as Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)—steer models to adhere to the Constitutional AI principles of Helpfulness, Honesty, and Harmlessness (HHH), while runtime guardrails enforce strict privacy, copyright, and safety boundaries.",
            part2Title = "Analytical Formulations, Worked Examples & Applications",
            part2Content = "Exact Equations & Governing Rules:\n• Cosine Similarity: cos(u, v) = (u · v) / (||u||₂ ||v||₂)\n• HNSW Graph Search: Greedily traverse multi-layer skip-list graphs to find nearest neighbors in O(log N) time\n• Equalized Odds: P(Ŷ = 1 | A = 0, Y = y) = P(Ŷ = 1 | A = 1, Y = y) for y ∈ {0, 1}\n\nWorked Numerical Example:\nProblem: Given a user query vector q = [1, 2] and two document chunk embeddings d₁ = [2, 4] and d₂ = [2, -1], determine which document is retrieved using Cosine Similarity.\nStep 1: Compute magnitude of query vector q:\n• ||q|| = √(1² + 2²) = √(1 + 4) = √5 ≈ 2.236.\nStep 2: Evaluate Document 1 (d₁ = [2, 4]):\n• Dot product q · d₁ = (1)(2) + (2)(4) = 2 + 8 = 10.\n• Magnitude ||d₁|| = √(2² + 4²) = √(4 + 16) = √20 = 2√5 ≈ 4.472.\n• Cosine Similarity cos(θ₁) = (q · d₁) / (||q|| ||d₁||) = 10 / (√5 × 2√5) = 10 / (2 × 5) = 10 / 10 = 1.0 (Exact parallel alignment!).\nStep 3: Evaluate Document 2 (d₂ = [2, -1]):\n• Dot product q · d₂ = (1)(2) + (2)(-1) = 2 - 2 = 0.\n• Magnitude ||d₂|| = √(2² + (-1)²) = √(4 + 1) = √5.\n• Cosine Similarity cos(θ₂) = 0 / (√5 × √5) = 0.0 (Orthogonal / completely unrelated).\nConclusion: Document 1 has similarity 1.0 and is selected as the top-1 context chunk for RAG augmentation.\n\nReal-World Applications:\nRAG systems are the industry standard for enterprise generative AI. Financial institutions use RAG to query complex internal regulatory documents and SEC filings. Healthcare clinical assistants use RAG to cite current medical journals without hallucinations. Customer support bots use local RAG embeddings to troubleshoot proprietary hardware manuals.",
            examTip = "University Exam Tip: Remember that Cosine Similarity measures the COSINE OF THE ANGLE between two vectors, completely independent of their lengths: cos(θ) = 1 means parallel (identical semantic meaning), cos(θ) = 0 means orthogonal (unrelated), and cos(θ) = -1 means opposite. Normalizing vectors to unit length makes dot product equivalent to cosine similarity!",
            mcqs = listOf(
